--TEST--
`Core\IO\File::truncate` asks for no capability of its own — the handle is the grant — and the two ways it can still fail land on either side of `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`'s split — spec § 14, `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The capability question was settled at `open`: `fs.write` was shown then, and
// a handle is the proof it was granted. So `truncate` checks nothing itself,
// and what is left is the mode the descriptor actually carries — which is the
// operating system's answer rather than a slot this class keeps.
Core\IO\File $seed = Core\IO::open("kept.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Write);
$seed->write("abcdefghij");
$seed->close();

Core\IO\File $in = Core\IO::open("kept.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
try {
    $in->truncate(0);
    echo "resized a read-only handle", "\n";
} catch (IOError $refused) {
    echo "refused: read-only", "\n";
}
// The half that matters: the refusal happened before anything reached the
// file, so the ten bytes are still ten bytes.
echo "intact: ", Core\IO::size("kept.txt") as string, "\n";
$in->close();

// A closed handle is a `RuntimeError` and not an `IOError`, for every other
// member's reason: nothing about the file went wrong, the program asked a
// question of something it had already given up.
try {
    $in->truncate(0);
    echo "resized a closed handle", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $closed) {
    echo "closed: truncate", "\n";
}

// And the same file, opened for writing, resizes — so what the first refusal
// named was the mode and not the path.
Core\IO\File $rw = Core\IO::open("kept.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
$rw->truncate(3);
$rw->close();
echo "resized: ", Core\IO::size("kept.txt") as string, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
refused: read-only
intact: 10
closed: truncate
resized: 3
